WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.663 --> 00:00:12.288
New York, Paris, Tokyo, Moscow, Seoul, Beijing, Delhi—more than 150 cities around the world have subways.

2
00:00:13.088 --> 00:00:15.510
A subway is an electric underground railway.

3
00:00:16.351 --> 00:00:20.333
It makes it easy to get around downtown and connects a city to the suburbs.

4
00:00:21.334 --> 00:00:24.696
The oldest subway, and one of the biggest, is the London Tube.

5
00:00:25.657 --> 00:00:30.320
The London subway is called the Tube because its tunnels are narrow and round like a tube.

6
00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:35.267
In fact, its tunnels are the smallest in the world, but the Tube is one of the longest

7
00:00:35.287 --> 00:00:38.449
subways, with 391 kilometers of track.

8
00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:43.032
The Tube was the world's first underground rail system.

9
00:00:43.913 --> 00:00:45.594
It was built in 1863.

10
00:00:46.635 --> 00:00:49.997
But why did the city of London decide to build a railway underground?

11
00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:55.400
Well, in those days, the streets of London were very busy and dirty!

12
00:00:57.001 --> 00:01:02.053
Back then, the cheapest way to get around town was the omnibus—a horse-drawn bus.

13
00:01:02.813 --> 00:01:06.895
Twenty people could sit inside an omnibus, but it wasn't a pleasant experience.

14
00:01:07.895 --> 00:01:11.136
It was crowded, and there was dirty straw on the floor.

15
00:01:11.657 --> 00:01:14.118
People complained a lot about riding in an omnibus.

16
00:01:15.278 --> 00:01:21.321
So in 1836 the London Times newspaper published suggestions to make the journey more pleasant.

17
00:01:22.121 --> 00:01:23.481
They included: 1.

18
00:01:23.842 --> 00:01:26.483
Sit up straight and don't take the space of two people.

19
00:01:27.803 --> 00:01:29.504
2. Do not sit on the straw.

20
00:01:30.036 --> 00:01:31.497
You are not in a pigsty.

21
00:01:33.317 --> 00:01:36.818
3. If you have a dog, make sure it is small and has a leash.

22
00:01:37.978 --> 00:01:41.860
4. Do not bring large parcelsㅡan omnibus is not a van.

23
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.321
5. Don't expect the omnibus conductor to have change; he is not a banker.

24
00:01:48.442 --> 00:01:51.543
6. Don't act like you are better than other passengers.

25
00:01:52.403 --> 00:01:55.664
If you can afford to ride a private horse and carriage, then do so!

26
00:01:57.588 --> 00:02:03.330
Riding inside the omnibus may have been bad, but stepping out of the bus and onto the London

27
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.310
streets was even worse.

28
00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:11.252
With thousands of horses trotting along, the streets were filled with—you guessed it—horse poo!

29
00:02:11.912 --> 00:02:16.533
The horse dung was so bad that people, often children, worked as crossing sweepers.

30
00:02:17.394 --> 00:02:21.255
Their job was to sweep in front of rich people as they crossed the road.

31
00:02:22.015 --> 00:02:25.776
You can imagine that if people could avoid traveling in the streets, they would!

32
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.681
So when the Tube started in the late 1800s, it was a great success!

33
00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:35.862
Back then, the first Tube carriages were long, narrow, and had no windows.

34
00:02:37.303 --> 00:02:42.104
The Tube's original designers thought there was nothing to see in a tunnel, so why have windows?

35
00:02:43.224 --> 00:02:46.105
By the twentieth century, the Tube had expanded all over London.

36
00:02:47.446 --> 00:02:52.367
During World War I and World War II, Londoners used the Tube stations as bomb shelters.

37
00:02:53.447 --> 00:02:56.388
London was one of the worst-bombed cities during World War II.

38
00:02:57.344 --> 00:03:02.045
People would buy tickets and never come out, because the Tube station was the safest place to be.

39
00:03:02.946 --> 00:03:09.707
From 1939 to 1945, nearly 63 million people stayed in 79 of London's Tube stations.

40
00:03:11.108 --> 00:03:15.369
Restaurants and libraries were set up underground, and performers entertained people.

41
00:03:16.769 --> 00:03:19.850
Even with modern technology, the Tube still has problems.

42
00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.151
There's always the danger of flooding from the Thames River.

43
00:03:24.371 --> 00:03:31.461
After giant flood gates were built in 1984, flooding greatly decreased, but some Tube stations still get flooded

44
00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:32.742
during thunderstorms.

45
00:03:34.322 --> 00:03:39.844
In the summers it is very hot inside a Tube carriage, and air conditioners can't be installed for

46
00:03:39.864 --> 00:03:40.464
two reasons.

47
00:03:41.324 --> 00:03:45.225
First, they give off too much heat, and second, the carriages are too small.

48
00:03:46.085 --> 00:03:50.267
The mayor of London offered a reward of ten thousand pounds for a solution to this problem!

49
00:03:52.007 --> 00:03:54.728
If you ever go to London, make sure you take the Tube!

50
00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.804
Look for a round red sign and go down the stairs.

51
00:03:59.865 --> 00:04:05.551
Buy a ticket, wait on the narrow platform, then get on the Tube—and remember you're sitting inside a

52
00:04:05.611 --> 00:04:06.833
part of London's history!
